d give the user  control to explore change now this isn't  really available in Python right so a  graph tool uses gtk windows so you can  forgive interactivity you might think  there's interactivity with Jupiter  notebooks or something like that but  with the size of these graphs you can  see that you're already in a huge  performance train as it is so just to  wrap up I want to conclude with some  thoughts about network visualization  sort of a contrast to how hard this  might be you know the the most important  thing and hopefully what you're seeing  is that layout and positioning is by far  the most important and layout precludes  you from having many nodes in your graph  so you need to create sub graphs this is  from Lane Harrison and she talks about  all these properties of graphs that you  can change you can add elements to  particularly visual properties and the  reason I bring them up is because all of  these things are available in raft well  you can change the vertex shape color  size even give the PI notes for edges  you can change the color size direction  so these tools are available to you but  the question is you know is it enough  this is the complete key phrase graph  ordered by week there's not a whole lot  of understanding that you can do in  these things and so you need to work on  it it needs to be interactive you need  to have a tool that you can go back and  forth and specify different levels and  modify it and with that I will leave you  with this idea of visual analysis and  graphs being essential for a visual  analysis where there's a connection  between the human and the analytical  procedures  and challenge you guys to all go and  look specifically how you can create  interaction inside of your analytics  with the mantra overview first zoom and  filter details and demand and if you can  do that I think that time and the  dynamics of grass will become some a  very powerful tool that you can use in  your analytics tool kits
 

